What Is The State?
The Nation-State
The nation-state is central to how we understand global politics today. When we think about
the world, it is divided into nation-states each with control over its own land and people. This type
of political unit shapes everything in global politics from war to cooperation to identity and
belonging. As a result, it is easy to assume that the world has always been understood in terms of
and divided into nation states. This has not always been the case, though.
The Nation State Wasn’t Inevitable
Global politics hasn’t always been understood in terms of nation-states; historically, various other
political formations have shaped how societies organised and governed themselves. We didn’t
always have nations, and we didn’t always have states.
● City-States: In Ancient Greece, politics was centered around individual city-states like
Athens and Sparta, which were small, self-governing communities that controlled
surrounding territories but operated independently from one another.
● Empires: Large empires, such as the Roman Empire or the Ottoman Empire, expanded
across vast territories, ruling diverse peoples under a central authority without the modern
concept of nationhood or fixed borders.
● Asian Conceptions of Statehood: In parts of Asia, political authority was historically
defined by the people under a ruler’s control, not the land itself. Loyalty and hierarchy,
rather than fixed borders, were the basis of political organisation. Examples include the
Mandala system in Thailand and tribute system in imperial China.
● Tribal Societies: Many pre-modern and indigenous societies, such as the Iroquois
Confederacy in North America, organised themselves into tribal or clan-based systems,
where governance was collective, and authority was based on kinship and consensus
rather than territorial control.
The Rise Of The Nation State
The Birth Of The Nation-State In The Treaty Of Westphalia 1648:
● The rise of the nation-state as the dominant political structure began in Europe, particularly
with the Treaty of Westphalia in 1648. The treaty ended the Thirty Years’ War, a
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 3
devastating conflict in Europe driven by religious and political rivalries between competing
kingdoms.
○ The chaos of the war demonstrated the need for a new system to maintain peace
and manage the relationships between these entities.
● The Treaty of Westphalia was signed, setting out that Europe was divided into sovereign
nation-states with clear borders, each having control over its territory and people; the
mutual recognition of states.
○ This marked the beginning of the modern state system and emphasised the
principle that states shouldn’t interfere in the domestic affairs of other states.
How European Colonialism Spread The Idea Of The Nation-State:
● As European empires expanded, they exported the nation-state concept through
colonialism. Colonised regions were often carved into artificial states with fixed borders,
irrespective of existing cultural or political structures.
○ This can be seen in the straight lines that mark many borders in Africa and the
Middle East in particular.
● Africa was literally divided up between European Empires in the 1884-85 Berlin
Conference.
How The Nation-State Became Truly Global Through Decolonisation:
● Though European colonialism exported the idea of the nation-state across the rest of the
world and spoke of sovereignty, in reality the empire was the dominant political formation
across the majority of the Global South.
○ Following the end of the Second World War, though, colonies in Africa, Asia and the
Middle East gained independence as nation-states and were recognised by the
United Nations.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 4
● These new states often struggled due to the artificial borders imposed by colonial powers,
which led to internal conflicts and challenges in state-building.
○ This difficulty was compounded by the fact that these new states largely remained
economically dependent on their former colonies and Western states, which were
able to exercise significant influence over them as a result.
Defining The Nation State
The nation-state as a political unit is defined by four central characteristics:
1. Defined Territory: A nation-state has clearly recognised borders within which it exercises
control. The territory is central to the state’s identity and sovereignty, distinguishing it from
other states. This territory is acknowledged by treaties, international law and other states.
2. Permanent Population: A nation-state consists of a stable population of citizens who live
within its borders. These people share certain legal, cultural, or historical bonds, which
contribute to the sense of national unity.
3. Sovereign Government: A nation-state possesses a centralised government that has
authority over its internal affairs, making laws and enforcing them independently.
Sovereignty means that the state is not subject to the authority of any other state or
external power.
4. Ability To Engage With Other States: A nation-state has the recognised authority to
engage in diplomacy, sign treaties, and participate in international organisations.
The ability to engage with other states is crucial because it underscores the importance of
international recognition in defining statehood. A nation-state’s sovereignty and legitimacy are
not just self-declared; they rely on recognition from the international community and other states,
particularly powerful ones that influence global politics. This international recognition defines who is
accepted as a nation-state and who is not. This is not always decided based on the above criteria,
but also on the interests of existing states.
● This can be seen in the recognition of South Sudan as an independent state in 2011.
International recognition, particularly from powerful countries, solidified its sovereignty and
status as a nation-state despite ongoing internal conflict.
● In contrast, Taiwan, despite having its own government, economy, and defined territory, is
not widely recognised as an independent nation-state due to pressure from China and the
strategic interests of major global powers.
○ China claims that Taiwan is a part of mainland China. As a result, Taiwan is
recognised by a majority of countries as a self-governing territory, rather than a
nation-state. It is not a member of the United Nations as a result.
Citizenship and Nationality
The distinction between citizenship and nationality is useful in helping to understand nation-states
and the challenges that often face them.
● Citizenship refers to a person’s legal status within a state, granting them rights and
responsibilities, such as voting, paying taxes, and receiving protection under the law.
● Nationality is more about cultural or ethnic identity, often tied to shared history, language,
or heritage.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 5
In a nation-state, citizenship typically aligns with nationality, as seen in countries like Japan, where
most citizens share a common national identity of being Japanese. However, this isn’t always the
case.
● In Switzerland people of different linguistic and cultural backgrounds (German, French,
Italian) all hold Swiss citizenship, but their sense of nationality may vary based on regional
identity.
● Palestinians may identify with a Palestinian nationality, even if they hold citizenship in other
countries, reflecting how national identity can exist independently of legal state recognition.
Nationality and identity can pose significant challenges to nation-states, as they often contain
multiple identities and nationalities within their borders, including citizens who share a nationality
with that of another nation-state. These challenges can lead to secessionist movements, internal
divisions and war which threaten the very existince of nation-states.
National Sovereignty
National sovereignty refers to a state’s absolute authority over its territory and the people within
it, free from external interference. It is a foundational principle of the modern international system,
ensuring that each nation-state can govern itself without outside influence. There are two key
dimensions to national sovereignty:
● Internal Sovereignty: This refers to the state’s ability to manage its own affairs, including
making and enforcing laws, maintaining order, and managing its economy. Internal
sovereignty ensures that the government has undisputed authority over its population and
institutions within its borders.
● External Sovereignty: This refers to the state’s recognition from other states and
international bodies. External sovereignty allows the state to engage in foreign policy, form
treaties, and participate in international organisations, without being controlled or dictated to
by other states/bodies.
