Case Studies In Relation To National Sovereignty
Case Study: The Russian Invasion of Ukraine and National Sovereignty:
The Russian invasion of Ukraine on February 24, 2022, was a severe violation of Ukraine’s
national sovereignty. Russia’s military incursion aimed to exert control over Ukraine’s territory,
citing security concerns and historical ties, and challenging Ukraine’s right to self-governance and
territorial integrity. The invasion directly undermined Ukraine’s sovereignty, as Russian forces
attacked and occupied key regions, including Donetsk and Luhansk.
Since the invasion, Ukraine has mounted a significant defence with the support of the international
community, receiving military aid from NATO countries and economic sanctions being imposed on
Russia. International bodies, such as the United Nations, have condemned Russia’s actions,
affirming Ukraine’s right to territorial sovereignty. However, the conflict has persisted, with Russia
attempting to annex occupied regions following referendums in these areas, further escalating the
sovereignty dispute.
The war highlights the tension between national sovereignty and external interference, with
Ukraine fighting to maintain its independence and control over its borders, while Russia seeks to
redraw borders. The conflict also underscores the significant role that global powers and
international recognition play in either defending or challenging the sovereignty of states.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 8
Case Study: Brexit and National Sovereignty:
The Brexit referendum in 2016, in which the United Kingdom voted to leave the European Union
(EU), was influenced by concerns over national sovereignty. Many advocates for Brexit argued that
the UK’s membership in the EU compromised its sovereignty, particularly in areas like immigration,
law-making, and trade policy. In these areas, the European Parliament and European Court of
Justice could overrule national laws in certain cases,
By regaining full control over its borders, laws, and trade policies, Brexit was seen as a reassertion
of the UK’s national sovereignty, with proponents arguing that it restored the UK’s ability to make
decisions without external interference from EU institutions. However, Brexit also posed significant
challenges to the UK’s sovereignty. The complex negotiations with the EU over trade, especially
regarding the Northern Ireland Protocol, showed that even outside the EU, the UK had to
compromise with other powers to maintain economic stability and peace in the region.
Furthermore, the economic impact of leaving the single market and customs union led to debates
over whether full sovereignty came at the cost of economic prosperity and global influence.
Overall, Brexit reflects the tension between sovereignty and regional cooperation, with the UK
prioritising independence from EU regulations and law-making, but facing new challenges in
managing its relationships and economy in the context of globalisation.
Realist and Liberal Interpretations Of The Nation State and Sovereignty
Realism and liberalism are the two most prominent theories used to understand global politics
relations. While both acknowledge the importance of the nation-state as a fundamental actor in
global politics, they diverge on several key points regarding the nature and role of sovereignty.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 9
Realist Interpretation
● Realists view the nation-state as the central and most powerful actor in an anarchic
international system, where no overarching authority exists above states.
● For realists, sovereignty is absolute, meaning that states must protect their autonomy and
security above all else. They argue that states exist in constant competition, pursuing
national interests, primarily survival, power, and security.
○ Realists argue that states must look out for themselves as cooperation is limited by
the inherent distrust and desire for power among states. Sovereignty must be
fiercely protected, as compromising it risks the state’s ability to survive.
● Realists are skeptical of international institutions or laws that constrain a state’s
sovereignty, seeing them as tools used by powerful states to maintain dominance rather
than genuinely cooperative mechanisms.
Liberal Interpretation
● Liberals, while also recognising the nation-state as an important actor, view the
international system as capable of cooperation and governed by interdependence.
● For liberals, sovereignty is important but not absolute; it can be voluntarily shared or pooled
to achieve mutual gains through international organisations and cooperation.
○ They believe that states can benefit from cooperation on global issues such as
trade, environmental protection, and human rights, and that sovereignty can coexist
with international law and institutions like the United Nations or the European Union.
● Rather than a zero-sum game where states are in constant competition, liberals see
international relations as a place where states can pursue peace and prosperity through
diplomacy and multilateral agreements.
Where They Agree
Both realists and liberals agree on the importance of the nation-state as a crucial actor in global
politics and on the significance of sovereignty in maintaining state autonomy and governance.
They also both acknowledge the existence of an international system where states interact and
sometimes cooperate.
Where They Disagree
The key difference lies in their views on the nature of sovereignty and international cooperation.
Realists emphasise the primacy of state sovereignty and the need for states to guard it against
external threats, while liberals believe that sovereignty can be shared through international
institutions to address global challenges more effectively. Realists are skeptical of IGOs like the
EU or UN, seeing them as potential threats to sovereignty, whereas liberals view them as tools for
managing interdependence and creating a more stable international order.
Globalisation
What Is Globalisation?
What Came Before Globalisation?
● Before the modern era of globalisation, the world was organised into largely isolated
nation-states, empires, and regional powers.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 10
○ There was interaction through trade routes like the Silk Road or colonial empires
that connected distant regions in a one-sided, hierarchical manner, but it was largely
limited to war, diplomacy and mercantile trade.
● There was much less interdependence in terms of culture, the economy or global
governance.
The Growth Of Globalisation
● The rise of industrialisation, capitalism and imperialism in the 19th century laid the
groundwork for increased global connectivity, as European powers colonised the rest of the
world, exploiting it for raw materials and labour.
○ Innovation in transport and technology furthered this process, which was
accelerated by the Second World War that required huge amounts of equipment,
people and food to be moved quickly across the globe. WWII made countries far
better at logistics.
● Globalisation accelerated further in the post-WWII period. International institutions such as
the United Nations, the World Trade Organization (WTO), and the International Monetary
Fund (IMF) were created, establishing a global framework for managing economic and
political cooperation.
● Improvements to global communications networks and the rise of the internet then
propelled globalisation into overdrive in the 21st century. They enabled instant connectivity
and a global flow of information, money and people.
Globalisation - A Complex Web Of Interconnectedness
Globalisation today is understood as a complex web of interconnectedness across multiple
dimensions, including economic, political, social, and cultural spheres, where actions in one part of
the world can have ripple effects globally. The factors driving globalisation are deeply connected,
reflecting the interlinking of people, countries, institutions, culture, economics, technology, and
politics:
● Social interconnectedness is evident in the global movement of people, driven by
migration, tourism, and education, as well as the rise of social media platforms that connect
individuals worldwide. These social interactions facilitate the sharing of ideas, values, and
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 11
lifestyles, fostering a more interconnected global society where people are increasingly
aware of and influenced by developments across borders.
● Countries are now more linked than ever through trade agreements, diplomatic
relationships, and military alliances, creating a global network of interdependence.
Multilateral organisations like the UN and regional bodies like the EU help mediate political
and economic cooperation, helping countries work together on issues like climate change,
conflict resolution, and public health.
● Technological advancements, particularly in communication and transportation, are key
drivers of globalisation. The internet, smartphones, and faster air travel have made it easier
and cheaper to connect with distant parts of the world, breaking down barriers to trade,
communication, and cultural exchange.
Political Globalisation
Political globalisation refers to the increasing interconnectedness and interdependence of political
systems across the world, leading to the development of global governance structures, the spread
of political norms, and the rise of international cooperation. As nation-states become more
interlinked, political decisions made in one country can have wide-reaching implications for others.
Key Aspects Of Political Globalisation:
Intergovernmental Organisations:
● Intergovernmental organisations (IGOs), in particlar the UN, are central to political
globalisation. These organisations provide platforms for countries to discuss global issues,
create binding agreements, and manage conflicts.
○ They facilitate cooperation and global governance on matters ranging from human
rights to trade, security, and environmental policies.
○ The Paris Climate Agreement of 2015 is a significant example of global political
cooperation to address the universal challenge of climate change. Under the UN
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 12
framework, nearly 200 countries committed to reducing greenhouse gas emissions
and taking action to limit global warming to 1.5°C above pre-industrial levels.
● These institutions often require countries to align their domestic policies with global
standards, thus influencing national sovereignty. For example, the United Nations Security
Council makes decisions on peacekeeping, sanctions, and military interventions, affecting
the autonomy of member states.
Supranational Organisations:
● Supranational organisations like the European Union (EU) represent an advanced form of
political globalisation, where member states voluntarily cede a degree of their sovereignty
to a higher political entity in exchange for the benefits of economic integration, political
stability, and shared security.
○ The EU makes decisions on behalf of its member states in areas such as trade,
immigration, and environmental policy, demonstrating how political authority can be
shared across national boundaries.
● This model of regional political integration is increasingly used as a blueprint for other
regions seeking closer cooperation, such as ASEAN or MERCOSUR.
Multilateral Diplomacy:
● Political globalisation has changed the nature of diplomacy. Multilateral diplomacy, where
multiple countries negotiate simultaneously, has become the norm in addressing global
issues.
○ For example, nuclear non-proliferation treaties, global health initiatives, and free
trade agreements require diplomatic cooperation across continents.
● This new form of diplomacy also includes soft power, where countries use cultural
influence, aid, and global prestige to achieve political ends. The United States and China
use their global influence not only through hard power (military force) but also through
diplomacy, economic aid, and cultural initiatives to project their political ideologies on a
global scale.
Global Political Movements:
● Political globalisation also includes the spread of global political movements, which
transcend national borders and advocate for universal causes.
● Varied movements including those promoting human rights, environmental protection,
gender equality, populism and democracy have gained momentum thanks to global
communication tools like the internet and social media.
Economic Globalisation
Economic globalisation refers to the increasing interconnectedness and interdependence of
economies across the world, driven by the expansion of international trade, investment, and the
global flow of capital, goods, and services. As countries integrate their markets, economic
decisions made in one part of the world can have significant implications for others. This process
has led to the rise of multinational corporations (MNCs), global supply chains, and international
financial systems that tie economies together more closely than ever before.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 13
Key Aspects of Economic Globalisation
Intergovernmental Organisations (IGOs):
● IGOs such as the World Trade Organisation (WTO), International Monetary Fund (IMF),
and World Bank are central to economic globalisation. These institutions regulate global
trade, provide financial assistance, and establish rules for international economic
cooperation.
● Their ability to provide financial assistance to struggling countries gives them huge
influence. During the mid-late 20th century, for example, the IMF and World Bank provided
loans to many subsaharan African countries on the premise that they would introduce
neoliberal reforms, which turned out to be disastrous.
Multinational Corporations (MNCs):
● Multinational corporations are key drivers of economic globalisation, operating across
borders and creating intricate global supply chains. Companies such as Apple, Amazon,
Tencent, and Toyota produce goods in multiple countries, sourcing raw materials from one
region, manufacturing in another, and selling globally.
● These corporations play a significant role in shaping global trade patterns, driving foreign
investment, and influencing domestic economic policies in countries where they operate.
They often have the power to pressure governments into adopting favourable tax policies or
regulations, which can challenge national economic sovereignty.
Global Trade and Finance:
● Global trade agreements, such as the North American Free Trade Agreement (NAFTA) or
its successor USMCA, and regional trade blocs like the European Union (EU) or ASEAN,
have further accelerated economic globalisation by removing trade barriers and promoting
the free flow of goods and services.
● Similarly, global financial markets have become increasingly interconnected, with capital
flowing across borders through foreign direct investment (FDI) and stock markets.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 14
● The integration of financial markets has brought prosperity for some, but it has also made
economies more vulnerable to global financial crises, such as the 2008 global financial
crisis, which had worldwide repercussions dispite originating in America.
Cultural Globalisation
Cultural globalisation refers to the increasing exchange and interconnection of ideas, values,
traditions, and cultural products across the world. This process has been driven by advancements
in communication technologies, international travel and migration, and the rise of global media,
leading to the diffusion of cultural practices, norms, and symbols across national boundaries.
One of the most significant debates in cultural globalisation is whether it leads to cultural
homogenisation - the dominance of a single global culture, often Western in origin, that erodes
local traditions - or cultural hybridisation, where local cultures blend global influences with their
own traditions to create something new.
Key Aspects of Cultural Globalisation
Cultural Exchange and Travel:
● International travel and migration have fostered cultural exchange and the blending of
cultural practices. People moving across borders for tourism, education, or work often carry
their traditions, foods, and customs with them, enriching the cultural landscapes of their
host countries.
● This can be seen in the global popularity of foods like sushi, pizza, and tacos, as well as the
increasing worldwide presence of cultural festivals like Chinese New Year and Diwali.
Global Media and Entertainment:
● Global media corporations such as Disney and Netflix have become powerful drivers of
cultural globalisation, spreading films, television shows, music, and other cultural products
to audiences around the world. This has led to the creation of a global entertainment
culture, where people from different countries consume and share the same media,
creating shared cultural references and experiences.
○ The global popularity of Hollywood movies, South Korean K-pop, and Bollywood
films illustrates how cultural products can transcend national borders, shaping
global cultural trends and tastes.
● Social media has also had a huge impact, allowing people to connect with others across
the world and more worldwide culture.
Global Consumerism and Brands:
● Multinational corporations like McDonald’s, Coca-Cola, and Nike are not just economic
actors but also cultural symbols, spreading global consumer culture. The presence of these
global brands in nearly every corner of the world represents the reach of Western consumer
