Globalisation has eroded some traditional
aspects of state control over their citizens,
particularly in areas like law and
regulation. As international human rights
norms, treaties, and supranational
organisations such as the European Union
(EU) and International Criminal Court (ICC)
gain influence, states increasingly find their
legal frameworks subject to global oversight
and their actions subject to international law.
This global legal integration means that
national laws must often align with
international standards, especially in areas
like human rights, environmental regulations,
and trade practices. While this ensures
greater protection for citizens, it also
challenges the state’s monopoly over
lawmaking and enforcement, reducing their
exclusive authority over their own legal
systems.
The EU represents a significant challenge to state
control over national laws. Member states must
conform to laws made by the European Parliament
and the European Court of Justice, which can
overrule national courts. For instance, the General
Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) is an EU-wide
law that regulates data privacy across all member
states, limiting individual countries’ ability to
legislate differently on the issue.
The establishment of the International Criminal
Court (ICC) challenges state sovereignty by
holding individuals accountable for war crimes and
crimes against humanity, even if those crimes were
committed within a sovereign state’s borders. In
2023 the ICC issued an arrest warrant for Putin and
in 2024 for Netanyahu for their actions in Ukraine
and Gaza respectively, highlighting how
international legal standards can challenge the
control of states over affairs within (and without)
their borders.
Political globalisations has led to an increase
in humanitarian and forcible interventions.
This refers to actions taken by states or
international coalitions, often under the United
Nations (UN) framework, to intervene in a
country’s internal affairs with the justification of
preventing or halting widespread human rights
abuses, civil war, or genocide.
These interventions, while sometimes justified
on the grounds of protecting civilians,
challenge the principle of state sovereignty by
overriding a nation’s control over its own
territory and internal matters.
